<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-agent-sandboxes"
      updated="September 9, 2026"
      evidence="Tested with Docker Sandboxes 0.39.0, nono 0.75.0, and Dev Containers CLI 0.89.0 on macOS on September 7, 2026."
      toc={[
        {"choose", "Choose a sandbox"},
        {"connect", "Connect"},
        {"coop", "co:op"},
        {"docker-sandboxes", "Docker Sandboxes"},
        {"nono", "nono"},
        {"dev-containers", "Dev Containers"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-agent-sandboxes">
        <:dek>
          We recommend running AI agents in a sandbox. It limits their access to files, secrets,
          and tools on your computer, while emisar extends that control to infrastructure and
          third-party tools.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          An emisar account with permission to connect an agent and at least one runner online.
          Complete the
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">quickstart</.link>
          first if you do not have a runner yet.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          A supported sandbox and coding agent from the table below, installed and ready to sign in.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="choose">Choose a sandbox</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        We recommend <strong class="text-zinc-200">co:op</strong> for most local setups. It is a
        free, open-source tool that supports Codex, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, and Grok CLI and lets
        you control which files, secrets, and tools the agent can access. It can also coordinate
        work across models and providers, switch providers during a session, and manage shared
        task queues.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[660px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="border-b border-zinc-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Sandbox</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Supported coding agents</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Good fit when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <a href="#coop" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">co:op</a>
                <span class="block text-xs font-normal text-zinc-400">Recommended</span>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">
                Codex, Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Grok CLI
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">
                You want precise control over which local files, secrets, and tools the agent can
                access.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <a href="#docker-sandboxes" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
                  Docker Sandboxes
                </a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">
                Claude Code, Codex, Devin, Gemini CLI, Kiro, OpenCode
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">You already use Docker's sandbox workflow.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <a href="#nono" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">nono</a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">
                Any terminal agent with a suitable profile
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">
                You want fast startup without a container and are comfortable using an
                operating-system profile as the sandbox boundary.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                <a href="#dev-containers" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
                  Dev Containers
                </a>
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-zinc-300">
                Any CLI agent installed in the container
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top">Your project already uses a Dev Container.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="connect">Connect a sandbox</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Connect an agent</a>, then choose your sandbox under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Agent sandboxes</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>Follow the steps shown there.</li>
        <li>
          Start the agent and send the example prompt. When the console shows <strong class="text-zinc-200">Agent connected</strong>, open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>
          to confirm the action, runner, operator, and result.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="coop">co:op</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="coop-requirements">What you need</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        co:op runs the agent in a container on macOS or Linux. Use Apple container, Docker, or
        Podman as the runtime. The console shows how to install the bridge in the image and keep
        its rotated credentials in a named volume.
      </p>

      <.docs_sandbox_risks id="coop-limits-and-risks" sandbox="coop" />

      <.docs_h2 id="docker-sandboxes">Docker Sandboxes</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="docker-sandboxes-requirements">What you need</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Docker Sandboxes runs supported coding agents in a microVM on a supported macOS or Ubuntu
        host and requires a Docker account. Its
        <.link
          href="https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/mcp-gateway/"
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >MCP gateway</.link>
        connects them to the emisar bridge on the host without copying the emisar key into the VM.
      </p>

      <.docs_sandbox_risks id="docker-sandboxes-limits-and-risks" sandbox="docker_sandboxes" />

      <.docs_h2 id="nono">nono</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="nono-requirements">What you need</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        nono can run any terminal agent under an operating-system profile on macOS or Linux. Use
        a suitable maintained profile from nono's
        <.link href="https://registry.nono.sh" class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          profile registry
        </.link>
        and keep the provider credentials where that profile expects them.
      </p>

      <.docs_sandbox_risks id="nono-limits-and-risks" sandbox="nono" />

      <.docs_h3 id="nono-rotation">Key rotation</.docs_h3>
      <.docs_sandbox_rotation sandbox="nono" />

      <.docs_h2 id="dev-containers">Dev Containers</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="dev-containers-requirements">What you need</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A Dev Container runs a CLI agent and the emisar bridge inside a project that already has a working
        <.docs_inline_code>.devcontainer</.docs_inline_code>
        setup. You need Docker and either the VS Code Dev Containers extension or the standalone
        Dev Containers CLI. Run the agent as a non-root user inside the container.
      </p>

      <.docs_sandbox_risks id="dev-containers-limits-and-risks" sandbox="dev_containers" />

      <.docs_h3 id="dev-containers-rotation">Rebuilds and rotation</.docs_h3>
      <.docs_sandbox_rotation sandbox="dev_containers" />
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
